WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
Start with the smallest useful state.

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:10.257
For the first learning build, four fields are enough: the question, retrieved documents, a step count,

00:00:10.257 --> 00:00:16.917
and the current route. Do not add a query field until you actually implement query rewriting.

00:00:16.917 --> 00:00:21.022
Do not add a score until you actually grade evidence.

00:00:21.022 --> 00:00:24.832
Do not add a draft until a generation step exists.

00:00:25.545 --> 00:00:29.457
This matters because a large state object can hide the workflow.

00:00:29.457 --> 00:00:37.977
Students sometimes create fifteen fields before the first node works, then spend most of their time moving values

00:00:37.977 --> 00:00:40.692
around. A better approach is incremental.

00:00:40.692 --> 00:00:47.772
Build the simplest loop. When a new node needs a value from an earlier step, add that field.

00:00:47.772 --> 00:00:55.234
For example, when you add an evaluator later, then it makes sense to add a critique field and a revision count.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:02.184
State should reflect the architecture you actually have, not the architecture you might build someday.
